## 1. SERP Intent Analysis

### 1a. Dominant Content Format

The top-ranking competitors split into two formats: comprehensive reference guides (DOJ, HUD, accessibility.com, Investopedia) and practical how-to explainers aimed at renters or property managers (disability law center, thehousingcenter.org, navigatehousing.com, mdaquest.org). The dominant format is an **informational reference guide** with clearly defined sections, covering protected classes, prohibited conduct, and disability-specific provisions. Many use H2/H3 structure with definition blocks and numbered lists.

This article should be a **structured reference guide with renter-facing practical framing**, not a listicle and not a pure legal reference. The guide format matches the informational intent while the renter framing differentiates brightplace from government sources and law firm content.

### 1b. Content Depth Benchmark

Competitors range widely:
- Short (government/law sites): 400-800 words of extractable content (heavy navigation bloat)
- Mid-range (navigatehousing, disabilityproud): 800-1,200 words
- Deep (nationaldisabilityauthority, Investopedia, justice.gov): 1,500-2,500 words

**Target word count: 1,100-1,400 words.** This exceeds the lightweight reference pages while staying tighter than the law-heavy deep dives. The searcher wants a clear answer, not a legal treatise.

